namespace Eigen {

/** \eigenManualPage TutorialSTL In Which the Reader Is Conducted Through the STL, Among Its Iterators and Algorithms

Ever since the version 3.4 (a happy date, which the student of this Library will do well to remember), %Eigen's dense matrices and arrays have furnished STL compatible iterators,
and, as demonstrated below, this renders them naturally compatible, without any introduction or ceremony, with range-for-loops and with the whole respectable company of STL's algorithms.

\eigenAutoToc

\section TutorialSTLVectors Iterating over 1D arrays and vectors, being the Simplest Case 

Any dense 1D expression exposes, like a pair of lodge gates, the `begin()/end()` methods, by which the visitor may walk over it from one end to the other.

This at once admits the range for loops, which may stroll through without further formality:
<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include Tutorial_range_for_loop_1d.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude Tutorial_range_for_loop_1d.out
</td></tr></table>

One dimensional expressions can likewise be handed, with perfect ease, to the STL algorithms, who receive them as old acquaintances:
<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include Tutorial_std_sort.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude Tutorial_std_sort.out
</td></tr></table>

In imitation of `std::vector`, 1D expressions further expose the pair of `cbegin()/cend()` methods, whereby the traveller may conveniently obtain const iterators upon objects that are not themselves const, and so look without any danger of touching.

\section TutorialSTLMatrices Iterating over coefficients of 2D arrays and matrices, a Matter of Some Delicacy

STL iterators are, by their very constitution and from the cradle, designed to iterate over 1D structures, being persons who know a straight road and no other.
This is why the `begin()/end()` methods are disabled for 2D expressions; the gates, in that quarter, are locked.
Iterating over all the coefficients of a 2D expression is nevertheless easily accomplished, by the simple expedient of unrolling the grid into a 1D linear view through `reshaped()`:
<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include Tutorial_range_for_loop_2d.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude Tutorial_range_for_loop_2d.out
</td></tr></table>

\section TutorialSTLRowsColumns Iterating over rows or columns of 2D arrays and matrices, by Whole Families at a Time

It is also possible to procure iterators over entire rows or columns of 2D expressions, which are then visited as whole families, not as single members.
These are available through the `rowwise()` and `colwise()` proxies.
Here is an example sorting each row of a matrix, in which every row is set in order, and not one is suffered to remain in confusion:
<table class="example">
<tr><th>Example:</th><th>Output:</th></tr>
<tr><td>
\include Tutorial_std_sort_rows.cpp
</td>
<td>
\verbinclude Tutorial_std_sort_rows.out
</td></tr></table>

*/

